  <name><text>Ch05_P05_Ex8_find_k</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>The exercise above: if \(n\) is an integer that is not divisible by 3, then \(n^2 = 3k + 1\) for some integer \(k\).</p>
<p>Write the two cases with a new letter, \(n = 3m + 1\) or \(n = 3m + 2\), where \(m\) is an integer. Find \(k\) in terms of \(m\) in each case.</p>
<ol type="a">
<li><p>If \(n = 3m + 1\), then \(n^2 = 3k + 1\) with</p>
<div>\(k =\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div></li>
<li><p>If \(n = 3m + 2\), then \(n^2 = 3k + 1\) with</p>
<div>\(k =\) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div></li>
</ol>]]></text></questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>To show that \(n^2 = 3k + 1\) for some integer \(k\), exhibit \(k\): expand \(n^2\) and write it as 3 times an integer, plus 1.</p>
<ol type="a">
<li><p>Case \(n = 3m + 1\): \(n^2 = 9m^2 + 6m + 1 = 3(3m^2 + 2m) + 1\), so \(k = 3m^2 + 2m\), an integer.</p></li>
<li><p>Case \(n = 3m + 2\): \(n^2 = 9m^2 + 12m + 4 = 3(3m^2 + 4m + 1) + 1\), so \(k = 3m^2 + 4m + 1\), an integer.</p></li>
</ol>
<p>A new letter for the case matters: \(k\) is a different integer, built from \(m\).</p>]]></text></generalfeedback>
